<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Publius Sittius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/44-bce">44 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Publius Sittius was a Roman adventurer from Nuceria who led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. During Caesar's African campaign in 46 BCE he invaded Numidia with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bocchus-ii">Bocchus II</a> of Mauretania and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>, forcing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I</a> to divide his forces. Caesar rewarded him with the lands around Cirta, which he settled with his followers, and he was killed in 44 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/publius-sittius.jpg" alt="Publius Sittius" width="320" height="149" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Publius_Sittius.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Publius Sittius came from the local elite of Nuceria in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>; his father, also Publius Sittius, had supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-220-217-bce">War of the Allies</a>. A Roman eques, he made his name as a financial operator, a businessman working across many places rather than a local banker, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Marcus Tullius Cicero</a>, who called him a friend, said he was owed enormous sums across the provinces and kingdoms. In 64 BCE he had his Italian affairs liquidated by Publius Cornelius Sulla to settle his debts and left to settle an account with the king of Mauretania.</p><p>After his departure he was suspected of sedition on behalf of Publius Sulla, a charge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> rejected in his speech Pro Sulla. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sallust">Sallust</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catiline">Catiline</a> told his fellow conspirators that Sittius was in Mauretania with an army and knew of his plan. Why Sittius really left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> is unclear; some authors link it to a proscription.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catiline">Catiline</a>'s defeat Sittius sold his Spanish and Italian holdings and stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, where he gathered a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> army chiefly of Italians who had been proscribed, embittered or ruined by the civil wars. He hired it out to North African rulers, changing sides when a better offer came, and grew rich and known as a skilled strategist. When the civil war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> and the Optimates began, his company was in the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bocchus-ii">Bocchus II</a>.</p><p>De Bello Africo, an account of the war by an unknown supporter of Caesar, records that after taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a> he defeated Juba's forces under Saburra, the victor of the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medjerda">Bagradas</a>. He ambushed Faustus Cornelius Sulla and Lucius Afranius as they fled towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> after Thapsus, and destroyed Metellus Scipio's fleet off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a>. The text never says that Caesar commanded him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On the lands Caesar granted him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>, Sittius founded a colony with his followers. He was killed in 44 BCE by Arabio, a son of the Numidian king Masinissa II. His territory was then annexed to the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa-nova">Africa Nova</a>, but his men's colonies kept their privileges. Epitaphs suggest that some came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a> and others from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and the family name Sittius became very common in the region.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Publius Sittius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Caesar made the core of the kingdom the province of Africa Nova, with Sallust as its first governor, while land around Cirta went to his ally <b>Publius Sittius</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nuceria, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.74,14.67&amp;year=-54" aria-label="Nuceria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4910 709 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Publius Sittius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5360 979h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5360 979h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.74,14.67&amp;year=-54">What was here in 54 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
